A||||7||jhar1987||scan tool||||1||
Z||000000||jhar1987||03-22-2000||11:18 PM||||what exactly does the scan tool tell you? is it different than your normal codes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-23-2000||01:01 PM||||The scan tools that I have seen in Pep Boy do little more than just ground the computer wrapped in a pretty plastic box. The big scanners will actually tell you the voltage reading of various sensors in addition to give you the acruate reading of things like the coolant temp, tach, etc. Actually a good investment if you can find one for a mid 80s ECM.||||reg||14
Z||000002||mwbackus||03-23-2000||02:11 PM||||The "code scan tools" the you see for like $20 are nothing more than elaborate paper clips.  Only misinformed people buy these things.  An ECM scan tool" is an actual instrument that allows the engine sensor data to be monitored in near real time;<P>PROM ID<BR>IAC valve - Steps<BR>Coolant Temp - °F<BR>Vehicle Speed - MPH<BR>MAP Sensor - Volts<BR>Engine Speed - RPM<BR>Throttle Postition Sensor - Volts<BR>Integrator - Count<BR>Oxygen Sensor - Millivolts<BR>Battery Voltage - Volts<BR>Block Learn - Count<BR>O2 Cross Counts - Count<BR>Spark Advance - °<BR>EGR Duty Cycle - %<BR>Manifold Air Temperature - °F<BR>Injector Pulse - Milliseconds<BR>Battery Voltage - High/Low<BR>Learn Control Mode - Enabled/Disabled<BR>Air/Fuel Ratio - Lean/Rich<BR>Loop Status - Open/Closed<BR>A/C Compressor - Requested/Not Requested<BR>Park/Neutral - Detected/Not Detected<BR>TCC Status - Locked/Not Locked<P>The Autoxray GM scanner is available from Checker Auto for about $150 (Summit wants $175.69, part no. AXY-10110). It's exactly the same as the $300 XP240, but only comes with the GM cable. (Cable for other vehicles can be purchased separately).<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-23-2000||03:05 PM||||In addition to what MWB said....<P>On ECM's with intigrated cruise control it also reports that data. (In fiero only 87-88 4 cyl has the Cruise in the ECM. It's external to the ECM in all the others.)<P>The AutoXray GM only unit will work on all domestic GM product from about 82-95 except certain cadilac models. a couple GM import and import based models, mostly Geo's also don't work with it.<P>With some later GM ECM's I think it can also do a reset on the ECM. This function is not available on Feiro.<P>If you have more than one type of computer controled car you might want to look at the AutoXray XP240 or one of the other unversal scanners. An XP240 setup will run around 250$us for the scanner box, and about 30$us each<BR>for the cables. This is less than most comparable scanners which will often run around 500$us for just enough for 2 brands of cars.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||05-29-2000||06:42 AM||||I'm resurecting this thread because I just bought an AutoXray EZ-Link Pro-Pack. It cost me $359 and comes with the software and cables to read all 96-present OBD II vehicles as well as GM, Ford, and Chrysler OBD I vehicles. I primarily wanted the OBD II function for my wife's 96 Pontiac Sunfire and the GM OBD I functions for my own 94 Suburban and 85 Fiero. I guess maybe I'm experiencing a slight case of buyers remorse for spending quite so much right now. Being as I personally only need the GM OBD I and OBD II scan capability, do you guys think this is a good deal? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-29-2000||11:58 AM||||that's about normal for a universal scanner.<P>The base AutoXray XP240 is/was $250 + $30 per cable set. The other ones in pep boys etc will run around $200something for the box then around 50-100 for each cable & rom pack.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BN Boomer||05-29-2000||03:27 PM||||Do they even still offer the XP240 or have they just changed the name to the EZ-Link? I was poking around AutoXray's website at <A HREF="http://www.autoxray.com" TARGET=_blank>AutoXray</A> trying to figure out if the base OBD II scanner that goes for $259 at my local store might have been a better deal and then just get the GM OBD I cable for another $30. I'd still be into it for $290 and I'm not even sure if that particular scanner has the OBD I software installed. Otherwise, that's another $80. To me, it looks like a total of an additional $108 for the software and cable for each additional OBD I application. I'll probably just wind up keeping the Pro-Pak as I have friends and family who own the OBD I Ford and Chrysler products.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-30-2000||12:49 AM||||At this point I'd stay with the one you have. No, I don't know if the XP240 is still built. It may still be in some inventories. I was just using it as an example.||||reg||1
